---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Snippet from '../components/Snippet.astro';
import { baseUrl, kb, loadRelease } from '../lib/site.mjs';

const base = baseUrl(import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
const release = loadRelease();
const kits = Object.entries(release.kits);
const basic = release.kits.basic;

const specExample = `{
  "data": "sales",
  "size": [640, 300],
  "transforms": [ { "type": "aggregate", "groupby": ["region"], "measures": [ { "op": "sum", "field": "total", "as": "sum" } ] } ],
  "scales": { "x": { "type": "band" }, "y": { "type": "linear", "zero": true } },
  "guides": [
    { "type": "grid", "scale": "y" },
    { "type": "axis", "scale": "x" },
    { "type": "axis", "scale": "y", "title": "Revenue", "format": { "style": "currency", "currency": "USD" } }
  ],
  "marks": [ { "type": "rect", "x": "region", "y": "sum" } ],
  "interaction": [ { "type": "tooltip" } ]
}`;
const formatExample = `{ "style": "currency", "currency": "EUR", "maximumFractionDigits": 0 }
{ "month": "short", "year": "2-digit" }
{ "maximumFractionDigits": 1, "suffix": " kg", "locale": "de-DE" }`;
const shell = `npm install
npm run build && npm run manifest
node scripts/compose.js skills/blockcharts/recipes/report-from-csv.json report.html`;
const integrity = `<script src="release/kits/basic.js"
        integrity="${basic.js.sha384}"
        crossorigin="anonymous"></script>`;
---
<Base title="Guide" nav="guide" version={release.version} description="How a chart spec works, how to feed data, and which files to ship.">
  <h1>Guide</h1>
  <p class="lead">The ideas in one page: a chart is a JSON spec; blocks do the work; you pick how the code reaches the page.</p>

  <h2 id="spec">A chart spec</h2>
  <p>A spec names a dataset and says how to draw it. There is no code in it.</p>
  <Snippet code={specExample} label="chart spec" />
  <ul>
    <li><b>Scales</b> are declared once and named: <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> by convention, anything else is yours (<code>color</code>, <code>y2</code>, …). A chart can have several scales in one direction, which is how dual axes work.</li>
    <li><b>Marks</b> read data through <i>channels</i>: <code>"x": "region"</code> means the field <code>region</code> on the scale named <code>x</code>.</li>
    <li><b>Guides</b> (axis, grid, legend) attach to scales, not marks, so two marks on the same scales share one axis.</li>
    <li><b>Transforms</b> prepare the data first: stack, bin, aggregate, decimate.</li>
    <li><b>Interactions</b> are zoom, brush, legend filter and tooltip.</li>
    <li>A block is named by its role and type; <code>{'{"type": "rect"}'}</code> in <code>marks</code> is <code>mark.rect</code>. <a href={`${base}blocks/`}>All blocks and their parameters.</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="data">Data</h2>
  <p>
    Datasets are named and shared: several charts can read the same one. Rows, columns, CSV (when composing), gzip and binary columns are all accepted;
    <a href={`${base}embed/data/`}>see them side by side</a>. Dates are ISO strings and are read as UTC. A missing value is <code>null</code>, and a row without a value is simply not drawn.
  </p>

  <h2 id="formatting">Numbers and dates</h2>
  <p>
    Axes, tooltips, text labels and color bars take a <code>format</code>: a preset (<code>percent</code>, <code>compact</code>, <code>integer</code>, <code>year</code>,
    <code>month</code>, <code>day</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>datetime</code>) or an object of <code>Intl</code> options, with <code>locale</code>, <code>prefix</code> and
    <code>suffix</code> on top. The default locale is <code>en-US</code> and dates are UTC, so a report reads the same on every machine.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={formatExample} label="format examples" />

  <h2 id="composer">The composer and the agent skill</h2>
  <p>
    The composer turns a <code>page.json</code> into one HTML file. It works out which blocks the charts need, checks every spec against the real data (each mistake comes back with
    its path in the JSON), draws every chart once in a sandbox to catch what only shows up when drawing, and writes the page. The same input always gives the same bytes, and nothing
    is written until every chart is valid.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={shell} label="shell" />
  <p>
    <code>npm run skill</code> packs the composer with instructions and recipes for an AI agent. The agent writes only JSON, runs the composer, and fixes what the messages say.
    Every <a href={`${base}examples/`}>example</a> is a recipe from that package.
  </p>

  <h2 id="files">Files, kits and sizes</h2>
  <p><code>npm run release</code> writes the files below. Everything is minified.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Kit</th><th>Blocks</th><th>Size</th><th>gzip</th><th>For</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {kits.map(([name, k]) => <tr><td><code>{name}</code></td><td>{k.blocks.length}</td><td>{kb(k.js.bytes)}</td><td>{kb(k.js.gzip)}</td><td>{k.doc}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Size</th><th>gzip</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>core.js</code></td><td>{kb(release.core.bytes)}</td><td>{kb(release.core.gzip)}</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>loader.js</code></td><td>{kb(release.loader.bytes)}</td><td>{kb(release.loader.gzip)}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Besides kits, every block is a file of its own (<code>blocks/mark.line.js</code>) and <code>manifest.json</code> lists them with their dependencies. A kit for one page:
    <code>node scripts/build-dist.js --page page.json=report</code>. Kits are also ES modules (<code>.mjs</code>); <a href={`${base}embed/module/`}>see the module demo</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pin">Pin a version, add integrity</h2>
  <p>Files of a version never change, so put the version in the CDN path. <code>manifest.json</code> has a SHA-384 hash for every file, ready for <code>integrity</code>. This is the real hash of the kit on this site:</p>
  <Snippet code={integrity} label="HTML" />

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Charts are SVG: made for report-sized data (up to a few thousand marks; more with the <code>decimate</code> transform). A Canvas renderer for very large data is planned.</li>
    <li>Modern browsers. Gzipped datasets need <code>DecompressionStream</code>. The composer needs Node 18 or later.</li>
    <li>Custom code (a tooltip that builds its own content) is registered with <code>BC.defineFn</code> and referred to by name, so specs stay plain JSON. <a href={`${base}embed/api/`}>Example</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
